{"title":"Regression","markdown":{"headingText":"Regression","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-regression","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\nRegression analysis and correlation analysis address the same fundamental problem: understanding relationships between variables. They diverge in purpose, interpretability, and analytical output. Correlation (@sec-correlation), typically quantified using measures such as the Pearson correlation coefficient or Spearman rank correlation coefficient, provides a standardized estimate of the strength and direction of association between two variables. Its value ranges from −1 to +1, indicating whether variables move together, inversely, or show no linear relationship. However, correlation is symmetrical and non-directional; it does not distinguish between dependent and independent variables, nor does it allow prediction. It answers a limited question: whether variables vary together and to what extent. \n\nRegression analysis extends this framework by introducing directionality and purpose. It explicitly models the relationship between an outcome (dependent variable) and one or more predictors (independent variables). In doing so, it enables:\n\n- Estimation of the magnitude of effect (how much change in outcome per unit change in predictor)\n- Adjustment for multiple variables simultaneously (control of confounding)\n- Prediction of outcomes under specified conditions\n\n In simple settings, the association captured by correlation is mathematically related to the slope estimated in Linear regression (@sec-linear-reg), but regression provides an explicit functional form linking variables. In applied medical and public health research, this distinction is operational: correlation serves as an exploratory tool, whereas regression supports inference, adjustment, and decision-making.\n\nWhen the outcome variable is binary, the assumptions of linear regression are violated, and alternative modeling approaches are required. Logistic regression (@sec-log-reg) addresses this by modeling the probability of an event using a logit transformation, producing estimates in the form of odds ratios. This allows interpretation in terms of relative likelihood while accommodating non-linear relationships between predictors and the probability scale. Logistic regression is therefore central to clinical and epidemiological research where outcomes are frequently dichotomous, such as disease presence or absence, treatment response, or mortality.\n\nBeyond these, a broader class of models—collectively termed Generalized Linear Models—extends regression to different types of outcome distributions through appropriate link functions. These include Poisson regression for count data, log-binomial regression for estimating risk ratios directly, and negative binomial regression for overdispersed count data. Each model is defined by a specific combination of distributional assumption and link function, allowing analytical flexibility when standard linear or logistic frameworks are inadequate. In situations where the outcome is time until an event, standard regression approaches are insufficient due to censoring and varying follow-up times. Methods from survival analysis, such as Kaplan–Meier estimation and Cox proportional hazards modeling, are designed to handle these complexities. These approaches focus not only on whether an event occurs, but also on when it occurs, providing a different but complementary perspective to regression-based analyses. As of writing of this volume these are out of scope and will be discussed in subsequent volumes.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":8,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":false,"output":true,"warning":false,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":fals